import sys; sys.path.insert(0,'..')
from con_helpers import *
save(2,1,"How the One Becomes the One You Call I","When a wave of feeling comes this week, reword it: not I am worried, but worry is here and I am the one noticing it. Feel whether even a thin gap opens.",[
mc("U","Sunlight fills the sky, then comes through a kitchen window as a beam on the floor. What does this picture show?",
 ["Localisation: awareness is organised around a centre, a here and a there, without being cut into pieces.","Limitation: awareness is made smaller, so that less of it is left after it has passed the window.","Identification: awareness takes the beam to be the whole of the sun and forgets the sky around it.","Division: awareness is split into many separate lights, each of which belongs to one person only."],0,
 "Sarah says it is the same sunlight, just given a frame. Awareness has not shrunk; it is shining through a particular window, and the window makes it feel local. On its own this is harmless and even necessary: you cannot function without a standpoint.",
 "Localisation gives a centre without dividing the light","Part 1"),
mc("A","James says that by the last period on a Friday he knows more than he can reach in that moment. Which idea does Swamiji give this?",
 ["Limitation, saṅkoca: awareness is contracted in expression, a vast thing working through a narrow pipe.","Localisation, in which awareness is spread evenly through the room and so cannot be called to one place.","Identification, in which a teacher takes a tired role to be the whole of who he really is for good.","Avidyā, a lack of facts which a teacher could make good with more study and a longer night’s sleep."],0,
 "The awareness is not destroyed or made smaller in itself. Sarah finds it comforting: the limitation is not her being inadequate, just the shape of being a person. It is what makes learning, surprise and growth possible, though it also makes the individual feel vulnerable.",
 "Saṅkoca: contraction in expression","Part 1"),
mc("A","Kai describes an actor so lost in a role that the person behind it is forgotten. What does Swamiji say this shows?",
 ["Identification: the point of view stops being an instrument you look through and is taken for what you are.","Localisation: the actor has found the centre of the stage, and so a here and a there appear for him.","Limitation: the actor has lost his skill, and so he can no longer attend to more than a few things.","Memory: the actor has learned his lines so well that he can no longer tell them from his own words."],0,
 "The costume is mistaken for the wearer. Localisation gave a centre and limitation gave finitude; identification gives ownership, so experience is no longer just happening but happening to me. This is the moment the jīva is born, the one who does, enjoys and suffers.",
 "Identification: the costume mistaken for the wearer","Part 1"),
judge("“Suffering takes root in being an individual at all, so the aim of the teaching is to destroy the personal self.”",F,
 "Swamiji says suffering takes root not in being an individual but in mistaking the individual for the whole of what you are. Distinguishing the two is the beginning of freedom, not because the personal self has to be destroyed but because it can be put in its proper place: a wonderful instrument and expression, not the final truth of you.",
 "The problem is mistaking the part for the whole","Part 1"),
mc("U","The lesson quotes the Pratyabhijñā Hṛdayam with the image of a great tree inside a tiny seed. What does the image say about a person?",
 ["Nothing is missing in you: you are the whole universe in seed form, contracted and not cut off.","You are a very small thing, which can grow into something larger only after a lifetime of work.","You are a tree that has already grown, so there is no more growing to be done by you at all.","You are a seed that has been planted away from the tree, so you must find your way back to it."],0,
 "The trunk, branches, leaves and fruit are all genuinely there, but folded down and condensed. Sarah notes that this feels very different from “I am just one small person.”",
 "The tree in the seed: contracted, not cut off","Part 1"),
mc("A","Sarah’s daughter says “I am anxious” and the whole of her seems to become the anxiety. How does Swamiji suggest Sarah responds?",
 ["Gently widen the frame: “anxiety is here right now, and you are the one noticing it.”","Argue the anxiety away, by showing her daughter that there is nothing at all to be anxious about.","Tell her daughter that the anxiety is a fault, which she should hide until it has passed on its own.","Ignore the anxiety, since a feeling that is not given attention will soon lose all its force."],0,
 "Arguing never works and tells her the feeling is shameful. The smallest gap between the feeling and the one who has it is enough. Acceptance and Commitment Therapy calls this defusion; the contemplative traditions have practised the same gap for thousands of years.",
 "Widen the frame: sky and weather","Part 2"),
mc("A","James notices he feels competent in class, fourteen again at his parents’ dinner, relaxed with a friend, and smaller alone at night. What does Swamiji point out?",
 ["Something was simply aware in all four rooms and never flickered; the four James-es are formations within it.","One of the four is the real James, and the other three are acts that he puts on for other people.","None of the four is real, so James is only a collection of masks with nothing behind them at all.","The four are the same James, so nothing about him changes from one room to another room."],0,
 "James says they are all him but none is the whole of him. The constant is closer to the true Self; the shifting selves are not fake but are constructed, conditioned and changing. Kai puts it as weather and sky.",
 "The constant awareness and the shifting selves","Part 2"),
judge("“Avidyā means a lack of facts, so a person overcomes it by studying more.”",F,
 "It is usually translated ignorance but does not mean lacking facts. It means misreading your own nature: the contracted awareness taking itself to be a separate, sealed-off self and forgetting the fullness it is the local expression of. It is the weather mistaken for the sky.",
 "Avidyā is misreading your nature, not lacking facts","Part 2"),
mc("A","Kai says a neuroscientist would explain the self as something the brain builds. How does Swamiji respond?",
 ["Science explains how a bounded, remembered self is built; the traditions describe the ground it works in.","Science is mistaken about the self, so its account of the brain has to be set aside before the ground is seen.","Science and the traditions say exactly the same thing in other words, so one account could replace the other.","Science cannot say anything about the self, since the self lies wholly outside the scope of all measurement."],0,
 "Swamiji says science tells you superbly how the radio is built and tends to go quiet on where the music comes from. They answer different questions, so they are companions and not rivals. He adds that individuality is experientially real, psychologically necessary, biologically supported and socially reinforced.",
 "Science explains the formation; the traditions the ground","Part 3"),
mc("A","A student asks what the sun and the pots of water show about people. Which answer fits Swamiji’s?",
 ["One sun, many reflections that differ in clear or muddy water: one Consciousness-Energy, many real centres.","One sun with many pots, each holding a separate sun of its own, so every person is a separate source of light.","One sun with many pots that are empty and unreal, so no individual person matters in the end of things.","One sun that is dimmed by the muddy pots, so the source itself is made less by each person’s own troubles."],0,
 "The source was never divided or dimmed. Kashmir Shaivism adds that a body-mind is not a passive pot but a living ripple of the source’s own self-expression. For Sarah this makes another person not really a stranger, so compassion becomes accurate and not a moral effort.",
 "One sun, many reflecting conditions","Part 3")])
